## 10. Limitations and Future Improvements

The current implementation uses a rule-based sensor fusion strategy.

It can select the correct motor action from valid sensor inputs, and the motor controller has been physically verified.

However, reliable autonomous obstacle avoidance has not yet been demonstrated.

Future improvements include:

1. Investigating ultrasonic sensor wiring, timing, and power stability.
2. Comparing ultrasonic readings before, during, and after motor operation.
3. Using a more reliable pulse-timing method if necessary.
4. Validating ultrasonic distance accuracy against measured reference distances.
5. Adding filtering and measurement confidence checks.
6. Repeating live sensor-driven left and right turning tests.
7. Performing controlled, low-speed ground tests after sensor reliability improves.

## 11. Conclusion

Experiment 06 successfully implemented a rule-based sensor fusion control system using ultrasonic and infrared obstacle detection.

The infrared sensors were physically tested, all six decision cases passed, and forward, left-turn, right-turn, and stop motor actions were physically verified using simulated sensor inputs.

The experiment also identified a significant limitation: ultrasonic distance measurements were unreliable during integrated operation, frequently causing the robot to stop.

Therefore, the core decision and motor execution modules have been validated, while reliable real-time autonomous obstacle avoidance remains future work.

This experiment provides a foundation for improving the robot's perception reliability and developing more advanced autonomous navigation capabilities.